|-
| PS5 || <math>Z(C) = K</math> || -- || (CS1), (CS3), (CS7) || (PS4) || <toggledisplay>By our choice, <math>Z(C)</math> is an [[abelian characteristic subgroup]] of <math>G</math>. Further, since <math>C \le H = C_G(K)</math> and <math>K</math> is contained in <math>C</math>, <math>K \le Z(C)</math>. So <math>Z(C)</math> is an abelian characteristic subgroup of <math>G</math> containing <math>K</math>. By maximality of <math>K</math> with respect to being abelian characteristic, <math>K = Z(C)</math>.</toggledisplay>
|}

===Proving the conditions for criticality===

Our goal is now to show that <math>C</math> satisfies the conditions for being a critical subgroup. Recall that we have established that <math>C</math> is a characteristic subgroup of <math>G</math> and that <math>Z(C) = K</math>. Also, recall that:

<math>H = C_G(K), \overline{H} = H/K, \overline{L} = \Omega_1(Z(\overline{G}), C = H \cap L, \overline{C} = C/K</math>.

We check the conditions one by one:

# [[Frattini-in-center group]] condition: We need to show that <math>\Phi(C) \le K</math>: For this, observe that <math>\overline{C}</math> is contained in <math>\Omega_1(Z(\overline{G}))</math>, which is elementary Abelian (it is contained in an Abelian group <math>Z(G)</math>, and is generated by elements of order <math>p</math>). Thus, <math>C/K</math> is elementary Abelian, and we get <math>\Phi(C) \le K</math>.
# [[Commutator-in-center subgroup]] condition: We need to show that <math>[G,C] \le K</math>: Modulo <math>K</math>, the image of <math>C</math> is contained in <math>\Omega_1(Z(\overline{G}))</math>, which is contained in <math>Z(\overline{G})</math>. Hence <math>[\overline{G}, \overline{C}] \le [\overline{G},Z(\overline{G})]</math>, which is trivial. Hence <math>[G,C] \le K</math>.
# [[Self-centralizing subgroup]] condition: We need to show that <math>C_G(C) \le C</math>: We do this by contradiction. Since the proof is somewhat long, we do it in the table below.

'''ASSUMPTION''': Suppose <math>Q = C_G(C)</math>, such that <math>Q</math> is not in <math>C</math>. 

{| class="sortable" border="1"
! Step no. !! Assertion !! Facts used !! Steps used (earlier parts) !! Steps used (this part) !! Contradiction-prone assumption used? !! Explanation
|-
| PSC1 || <math>Q</math> is normal in <math>G</math> || (Char1), (Norm3) || (PS3) || -- || no || <toggledisplay>By (PS3), <math>C</math> is characteristic in <math>G</math>, hence by (Char1), <math>C</math> is normal in <math>G</math>. By (Norm3), we get that <math>Q = C_G(C)</math> is also normal in <math>G</math>.</toggledisplay>
|-
| PSC2 || <math>Q \cap C = K</math> || -- || (PS5) ||-- || no || <toggledisplay>By definition, <math>Q \cap C = C_G(C) \cap C = Z(C)</math>. We showed that <math>Z(C) = K</math>, so <math>Q \cap C = K</math>.</toggledisplay>
|-
| PSC3 || <math>\overline{Q} = Q/K</math> is nontrivial and normal in <math>\overline{G}</math> || (Norm1) || -- || (PSC1) || yes || <toggledisplay>Define <math>\overline{Q} = Q/K</math>. By (Norm1), and the fact that <math>Q</math> is normal in <math>G</math>, we obtain that <math>\overline{Q}</math> is normal in <math>\overline{G}</math>. Also, since <math>Q</math> is not contained in <math>C</math> (by assumption), <math>K</math> is a proper subgroup of <math>Q</math>, so <math>\overline{Q}</math> is nontrivial.</toggledisplay>
|-
| PSC4 || <math>\overline{Q} \cap \overline{C}</math> is trivial || -- || -- || (PSC2) || no || <toggledisplay><math>\overline{Q} \cap \overline{C}</math> is the same as the image in <math>G/K</math> of <math>Q \cap C</math>, because they both contain <math>K</math>. By (PSC2), this image is trivial.</toggledisplay>
|-
| PSC5 || <math>\overline{Q}</math> intersects <math>\overline{L}</math> nontrivially || fact (1) about Omega-1 || -- || (PSC3) || yes || <toggledisplay>Since <math>\Omega_1(Z(\overline{G}))</math> is normality-large in <math>\overline{G}</math> (fact (1) about Omega-1), and <math>\overline{Q}</math> is nontrivial and normal in <math>\overline{G}</math> (by PSC3), we conclude that <math>\overline{Q}</math> must intersect <math>\Omega_1(Z(\overline{G})) = \overline{L}</math> nontrivially.</toggledisplay>
|-
| PSC6 || <math>Q \le H</math>, and thus, <math>\overline{Q} \le \overline{H}</math> || -- || -- || -- || no || <toggledisplay>Since <math>K \le C</math>, we have <math>Q = C_G(C) \le C_G(K) = H</math>, so <math>Q \le H</math>. Going modulo <math>K</math>, we get <math>\overline{Q} \le \overline{H}</math>.</toggledisplay>
|-
| PSC7 || <math>\overline{C} = \overline{H} \cap \overline{L}</math> || -- || (CS3), (CS6),(CS7) || -- || no || <toggledisplay>By definition, <math>C = H \cap L</math>. Since all three subgroups contain <math>K</math> by definition, the intersection holds when we take quotients by <math>K</math>.</toggledisplay>
|- 
| PSC8 || <math>\overline{Q} \cap (\overline{H} \cap \overline{L})</math> is trivial || -- || -- || (PSC4), (PSC7) || no  ||<toggledisplay><math>\overline{Q} \cap (\overline{H} \cap \overline{L}) = \overline{Q} \cap \overline{C}</math> by (PSC7). By (PSC4), this intersection is trivial.</toggledisplay>
|-
| PSC9 || <math>(\overline{Q} \cap \overline{H}) \cap \overline{L}</math> is nontrivial || -- || -- || (PSC5), (PSC6) || yes || <toggledisplay>By (PSC6), <math>(\overline{Q} \cap \overline{H}) \cap \overline{L} = \overline{Q} \cap \overline{L}</math>, and (PSC5), this is nontrivial.</toggledisplay>
|-
| PSC10 || Contradiction! || -- || - || (PSC8) and (PSC9) || yes || <toggledisplay>(PSC8) claims that a particular triple intersection is trivial, while (PSC9) claims that the same intersection is nontrivial. We thus get a contradiction.</toggledisplay>
|}

